  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday morning through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 319 PM EDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nAs the long term period begins on Friday morning, the axis of an \nupper level ridge will be centered roughly along the Mississippi \nRiver Valley. This ridge will gradually shift eastward and amplify \nover the course of the day on Friday and into Saturday. Temperatures \nwill continue their steady climb underneath the ridge, with \nafternoon highs on Friday forecast to be in the upper 90s in areas \nsouth of I-85 and in the mid 90s to the north (excluding the far \nnortheast mountains). Deep moisture lingering across the area will \ncontribute to dewpoints in the low to mid 70s and precipitable water \nvalues between 1.8-2.2 inches. This combination of heat and humidity \nwill contribute to heat index values in the triple digits across the \nmajority of the area, with values between 105 and 109 in portions of \ncentral and east Georgia, which will warrant the issuance of another \nHeat Advisory for Friday afternoon. High temperatures will be a \ncouple of degrees warmer areawide on Saturday, which could bring \nheat indices across the majority of the forecast area above Heat \nAdvisory criteria and allow locations in east-central Georgia to \nflirt with heat indices of 110 degrees.\n\nMid-range model guidance continues to indicate that the ridge will \nhelp suppress precipitation chances through the weekend. However, it \nshould be noted that these models tend to overestimate the effects \nof mixing under the ridge in the summer with deep moisture also \npresent under the ridge, while underestimating the persistent \nnorthwesterly flow on the periphery of the ridge and how it can \nfavor MCS development and growth. Thus, at least isolated \nthunderstorms will be possible each afternoon and evening \nconsidering both the possibility of an active diurnal convective \npattern amid rising temperatures and the possibility of MCS \ndevelopment. Stronger storms that develop could produce gusty winds \nand locally heavy rainfall. On the flip side, showers and storms \nthat occur could provide some relief from the heat and prevent the \nhighest heat indices from being reached.",
